{"format_version":"vbulletin-3+-thread","url":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-classic-world-of-darkness/vampire-the-masquerade/1393587-old-people-which-edition-did-celerity-rules-the-best","id":1393587,"title":"Old people: Which edition did Celerity rules the best?","path":["Home","Onyx Path","White Wolf Worlds","World of Darkness","Vampire: The Masquerade"],"posts":[{"date":"2020-06-02T00:39:00-07:00","id":1393587,"title":"Old people: Which edition did Celerity rules the best?","content":"I've noticed that Celerity has changed drastically in every edition of VTM. It had new rules in V1, V2, revised, V20 and DAV20. <br /> <br /> For those that tried them all: Which edition of VTM did it best?","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":3}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/25794-mywifeisscary","name":"MyWifeIsScary","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar25794_2.png","title":"Banned","rank":0,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2019"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"3711"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2020-06-02T01:18:26-07:00","id":1393592,"title":"","content":"DAV20 for sure......But I always house ruled that it would always cost one blood point...","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":1}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/2582-gangrel44","name":"Gangrel44","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar2582_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Mar 2014"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"151"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2020-06-02T01:26:16-07:00","id":1393593,"title":"","content":"I'm pretty sure DAV Revised is technically different as well.<br /> <br /> That said, I think DAV20 has the best version, though it's highly dependent on DAV20's implementation of multiple actions in general.<br /> <br /> Early VtM with 1 vitae for Celerity extra actions was just too much, and the spend for the next turn aspect of it acted as more of a binary &quot;you have Celerity so spam it,&quot; or not than a real tactical control on the Discipline.<br /> <br /> The later moved to 1 vitae per extra action was good move in terms of making Celerity less dominating in a fight, and having some passive bonuses gave it some nice non-combat uses. However the Generation limit breaking aspect of it is clunky.<br /> <br /> DAV20 has a good mix here. It returns to 1 bp (two for both activated powers) to activate removing the need for the limit breaking spending, while keeping the passive bonuses, and not having it's effect on extra actions be overwhelmingly good.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/617-heavy-arms","name":"Heavy Arms","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar617_2.jpeg","title":"Banned","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"12634"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2020-06-02T01:59:52-07:00","id":1393596,"title":"","content":"I liked the Revised version the best, as it was the most fun and the most versatile imo,<br /> <br /> people would complain it was too powerful, and open to abuse and...compared to other combat disciplines, it definitely were powerful,<br /> but it was outclassed by mental and magical disciplines (like obfuscate and presence)<br /> plus, celerity was only really good if you had good stats or other combat disciplines to combine it with<br /> <br /> I have to add that I like what they did with celerity in v5, but the fact that you can take only one power per level is a deal breaker for me","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/><b>-</b>","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":3}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/10998-pleiades","name":"Pleiades","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar10998_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Jan 2016"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"1844"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2020-06-02T02:07:11-07:00","id":1393597,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Gangrel44</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-classic-world-of-darkness/vampire-the-masquerade/1393587-old-people-which-edition-did-celerity-rules-the-best?p=1393592#post1393592\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">DAV20 for sure......But I always house ruled that it would always cost one blood point...</div> </div> </div> </div><br /> Honestly I felt a most people would pick DAV20 and was wondering if I should ask for a second favourite.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":1}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/25794-mywifeisscary","name":"MyWifeIsScary","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar25794_2.png","title":"Banned","rank":0,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2019"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"3711"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2020-06-02T03:47:47-07:00","id":1393605,"title":"","content":"Second favorite?<br /> <br /> DAV Rev., with V20 being a close third.<br /> <br /> DAV Rev. doesn't have the passive oomph of V20, but works in the Revised multiple action rules which are less punishing to non-magical extra actions. As well, DAV Rev. doesn't have the &quot;all magical actions take places at the end of the round except defenses,&quot; thing, and instead everyone, magical or normal actions, cycles through one attack/misc. action, using up actions for defense as necessary, and around you go until everyone's out. This makes for more enjoyable fights in general rather than &quot;take all your mundane actions, wait for everyone else to take all their mundane actions, then take all your Celerity actions, and wait while everyone else takes all theirs.&quot;<br /> <br /> The utility of V20's passive boosts are nice (though also easily ported), but ultimately less fun than DAV Rev's overall package.<br /> <br /> It's almost like the DA cores get to learn for their related core VtM edition's mistakes and be better....","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/617-heavy-arms","name":"Heavy Arms","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar617_2.jpeg","title":"Banned","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"12634"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2020-06-02T05:48:45-07:00","id":1393610,"title":"","content":"I like Celerity in 3rd edition, it was a very powerful discipline combined with any other combat discipline (Potence, Protean or Quietus). But I even like it more in 5th edition, as you have lot of options.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":2}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/24202-justycar","name":"Justycar","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar24202_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"May 2018"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"320"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2020-06-02T07:00:01-07:00","id":1393622,"title":"","content":"&quot;Old People&quot;? The term fledgling, is &quot;elder&quot;, or in my case &quot;methuselah&quot;. In my opinion, 5th edition. I think they fixed it, in previous editions, celerity started to dictate conflict, despite each editions effort to remedy it, V20 came close to remedying it. In early editions it went something like this: Ventrue tries dominate (fails/resisted). Brujah with his 4 extra full actions grapples, bites and eats Ventrue.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":8}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/24582-ruthven","name":"Ruthven","avatar":"data:image/svg+xml;base64,","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Aug 2018"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"118"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2020-06-02T07:41:06-07:00","id":1393627,"title":"","content":"V5. The old celerity always was a &quot;rocket tag&quot; power.It was by far the most powerful physical discipline, coupled with Potence and aggravated damage from Protean it trivialized combat, giving it to NPCs was risky because you could kill PCs as well. Besides that it actually slowed down play because you needed to check on a bucket of dices. Right now celerity is steamlined and still good to have (some powers more than others of course) without being OP.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":5}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/24992-haquim","name":"Haquim","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar24992_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Jan 2019"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"523"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2020-06-02T07:48:18-07:00","id":1393631,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Heavy Arms</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-classic-world-of-darkness/vampire-the-masquerade/1393587-old-people-which-edition-did-celerity-rules-the-best?p=1393605#post1393605\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">Second favorite?<br /> <br /> It's almost like the DA cores get to learn for their related core VtM edition's mistakes and be better....</div> </div> </div> </div>But Core editions get to learn from previous core editions and be better.... Also I think DA games have had their share of &quot;Wow this works great in modern nights let's fuck it up&quot; <br /> <br /> <br /> The core of DAV20's goodness is that it halves the number of attacks you can make with celerity extra actions (unless you're using a ranged weapon) which is quite easy to port to the usual dice splitting systems. <br /> <br /> I mostly made the thread because I was wondering i people really had a soft spot for the edition where celerity blood expenditure counted against maximum generation and they'd have to charge up bursts of crazy celerity.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":3}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/25794-mywifeisscary","name":"MyWifeIsScary","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar25794_2.png","title":"Banned","rank":0,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2019"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"3711"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2020-06-02T07:56:42-07:00","id":1393635,"title":"","content":"I think that if you remove Rage as a thing that Garou did (i.e OP Celerity) and remove it from vampires, then you have a much more balanced game.<br /> <br /> Maybe one extra-attack at Level 5, not 5 extra attacks.","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/>Author of <i><a href=\"https://www.amazon.com/Cthulhu-Armageddon-C-T-Phipps-ebook/dp/B01KUOM7SI/ref=sr_1_1?s=digital-text&amp;ie=UTF8&amp;qid=1535982844&amp;sr=1-1&amp;keywords=Cthulhu+Armageddon\" target=\"_blank\">Cthulhu Armageddon</a></i>, <i><a href=\"https://www.amazon.com/Teenage-Weredeer-Bright-Falls-Mysteries-ebook/dp/B075TTN2FN/ref=sr_1_2?s=digital-text&amp;ie=UTF8&amp;qid=1535982717&amp;sr=1-2&amp;keywords=Weredeer#customerReviews\" target=\"_blank\">I was a Teenage Weredeer</a></i>, <i><a href=\"https://www.amazon.com/Straight-Outta-Fangton-Comedic-Vampire-ebook/dp/B01KNHICCC/ref=sr_1_1?s=digital-text&amp;ie=UTF8&amp;qid=1535982774&amp;sr=1-1&amp;keywords=Straight+Outta+Fangton\" target=\"_blank\">Straight Outta Fangton</a></i>, <i><a href=\"https://www.amazon.com/Lucifers-Star-C-T-Phipps-ebook/dp/B01MDM9MCT/ref=mt_kindle?_encoding=UTF8&amp;me=&amp;qid=\" target=\"_blank\">Lucifer's Star</a>, </i>and the <i><a href=\"https://www.amazon.com/Rules-Supervillainy-Saga-Book-ebook/dp/B07MB89S33/\" target=\"_blank\">Supervillainy Saga</a>.</i><br /> <br /> <a href=\"http://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/onyx-path-announcements/206-forum-rules\" target=\"_blank\"><b>Forum Terms of Use</b></a><br /> <a href=\"http://forum.theonyxpath.com/contact-us\" target=\"_blank\">the Contact Us link</a>.","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":2}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/12002-ctphipps","name":"CTPhipps","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar12002_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Jun 2016"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"13933"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2020-06-02T08:51:05-07:00","id":1393646,"title":"","content":"<span style=\"color:#252C2F\"><span style=\"font-family:Helvetica\"><span style=\"font-size:13px\"> whilst I applaud that <b>V5's &quot;Celerity&quot; is far more balanced than prior editions</b>, it's more a lose collection of highly specific speed-based gimmicks than an actual discipline that works on a principle a vampire builds up. Celerity can be infered as a discipline where you speed up time around yourself to do things super quickly without worrying about the forces of physics tearing you a new one. Messing with time for speed is really easy to put into a computer game (VTMB does celerity really, really well) but kinda difficult to simulate that with a table top. </span></span></span><br /> <br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>CTPhipps</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-classic-world-of-darkness/vampire-the-masquerade/1393587-old-people-which-edition-did-celerity-rules-the-best?p=1393635#post1393635\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">I think that if you remove Rage as a thing that Garou did (i.e OP Celerity) and remove it from vampires, then you have a much more balanced game.<br /> <br /> Maybe one extra-attack at Level 5, not 5 extra attacks.</div> </div> </div> </div>I think the issue with werewolves is that the idea that they're fast rage-monsters that claw at you in a dragon-ball like blur is kinda integral to their identity. It's almost literally half of what makes them who they are. There's lots of room to tone it down, but no room to have it gone. <br /> <br />","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":3}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/25794-mywifeisscary","name":"MyWifeIsScary","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar25794_2.png","title":"Banned","rank":0,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2019"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"3711"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2020-06-02T09:02:43-07:00","id":1393649,"title":"","content":"DAV20 is pretty good, but gets a little complicated with the only half your extra actions rounded up can be used for attacks. You're also still having to figure out the number of dice to drop from each roll, like splitting actions. My initial reaction was that it's needlessly complex, but got to like it from the balance perspective. A part of me still recoils at how much crap there is to keep track with it when it used to just be spend blood get extra actions. So my feelings are kind of mixed, but I've been sticking with DAV20 for my games lately. I feel like I have to reexplain splitting dice pools any time they come up no matter what group I'm playing with and it's always been a piece people have had trouble understanding about the WOD games.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":2}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/14519-drhappyangry","name":"DrHappyAngry","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar14519_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Aug 2017"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"233"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2020-06-02T09:09:40-07:00","id":1393650,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>MyWifeIsScary</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-classic-world-of-darkness/vampire-the-masquerade/1393587-old-people-which-edition-did-celerity-rules-the-best?p=1393646#post1393646\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">I think the issue with werewolves is that the idea that they're fast rage-monsters that claw at you in a dragon-ball like blur is kinda integral to their identity. It's almost literally half of what makes them who they are. There's lots of room to tone it down, but no room to have it gone.</div> </div> </div> </div>yeah, problem is that werf's rage and celerity in general don't translate that feeling of getting torn apart in a lightening fast flurry,<br /> quite the opposite, it makes the whole thing feel slow<br /> <br /> you take your turn, punch the garou, let's say it takes 20 seconds,<br /> then the garou takes his 5 turns, now the whole thing lasts more than a minute while you're waiting, doesn't feel dragon-ball like blur<br />","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/><b>-</b>","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/10998-pleiades","name":"Pleiades","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar10998_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Jan 2016"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"1844"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2020-06-02T09:30:10-07:00","id":1393654,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>MyWifeIsScary</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-classic-world-of-darkness/vampire-the-masquerade/1393587-old-people-which-edition-did-celerity-rules-the-best?p=1393646#post1393646\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">I think the issue with werewolves is that the idea that they're fast rage-monsters that claw at you in a dragon-ball like blur is kinda integral to their identity. It's almost literally half of what makes them who they are. There's lots of room to tone it down, but no room to have it gone.<br /></div> </div> </div> </div>Then their identity is bad for my games. Good for people who like it but I don't think I'd ever let it at my table. Probably why I could never get into Apocalypse like I could Forsaken.","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/>Author of <i><a href=\"https://www.amazon.com/Cthulhu-Armageddon-C-T-Phipps-ebook/dp/B01KUOM7SI/ref=sr_1_1?s=digital-text&amp;ie=UTF8&amp;qid=1535982844&amp;sr=1-1&amp;keywords=Cthulhu+Armageddon\" target=\"_blank\">Cthulhu Armageddon</a></i>, <i><a href=\"https://www.amazon.com/Teenage-Weredeer-Bright-Falls-Mysteries-ebook/dp/B075TTN2FN/ref=sr_1_2?s=digital-text&amp;ie=UTF8&amp;qid=1535982717&amp;sr=1-2&amp;keywords=Weredeer#customerReviews\" target=\"_blank\">I was a Teenage Weredeer</a></i>, <i><a href=\"https://www.amazon.com/Straight-Outta-Fangton-Comedic-Vampire-ebook/dp/B01KNHICCC/ref=sr_1_1?s=digital-text&amp;ie=UTF8&amp;qid=1535982774&amp;sr=1-1&amp;keywords=Straight+Outta+Fangton\" target=\"_blank\">Straight Outta Fangton</a></i>, <i><a href=\"https://www.amazon.com/Lucifers-Star-C-T-Phipps-ebook/dp/B01MDM9MCT/ref=mt_kindle?_encoding=UTF8&amp;me=&amp;qid=\" target=\"_blank\">Lucifer's Star</a>, </i>and the <i><a href=\"https://www.amazon.com/Rules-Supervillainy-Saga-Book-ebook/dp/B07MB89S33/\" target=\"_blank\">Supervillainy Saga</a>.</i><br /> <br /> <a href=\"http://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/onyx-path-announcements/206-forum-rules\" target=\"_blank\"><b>Forum Terms of Use</b></a><br /> <a href=\"http://forum.theonyxpath.com/contact-us\" target=\"_blank\">the Contact Us link</a>.","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/12002-ctphipps","name":"CTPhipps","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar12002_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Jun 2016"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"13933"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2020-06-02T09:39:23-07:00","id":1393657,"title":"","content":"I'll be the odd old Methuselah. The various LARP versions, which all broke Celerity down into distinct powers, but still kept a couple of extra action tricks. You could get an initiative bonus, a bonus to ties in tests based on your speed, and up to two extra actions as well as extra steps. It worked well and I'm glad that V5 went with the various speed and dexterity-based powers in their spread.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":4}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/1808-elmerg","name":"elmerg","avatar":"data:image/svg+xml;base64,","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Dec 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"539"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2020-06-02T10:22:51-07:00","id":1393665,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Pleiades</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-classic-world-of-darkness/vampire-the-masquerade/1393587-old-people-which-edition-did-celerity-rules-the-best?p=1393650#post1393650\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\"><br /> yeah, problem is that werf's rage and celerity in general don't translate that feeling of getting torn apart in a lightening fast flurry,<br /> quite the opposite, it makes the whole thing feel slow<br /> <br /> you take your turn, punch the garou, let's say it takes 20 seconds,<br /> then the garou takes his 5 turns, now the whole thing lasts more than a minute while you're waiting, doesn't feel dragon-ball like blur</div> </div> </div> </div>Some kind of static attack and damage bonus might speed things up, but i'm not good with crunch and would like to hear if thats feasible.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/3399-hachiman","name":"hachiman","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar3399_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":0,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Jun 2014"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"78"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2020-06-02T10:55:32-07:00","id":1393675,"title":"","content":"Personally, I think Celerity in Pre-V5 would work fine if it worked just like V20 Potence (i.e. add dice to Dex actions, spend a blood point to make those dice into automatic successes that turn) and clarify that successes are added after dice pools are split for multiple actions (vs. being limited by the lowest dice pool employed that turn).<br /> <br /> Given how V20\u2019s multiple action and defenses rules work, being able to add 1-5 successes during your turn to one or more physical actions is a pretty potent ability.<br /> <br /> I\u2019d also apply the same to Rage in Werewolf if doing a crossover; spend 1 rage to add half permanent rage successes to dex-based actions this turn.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/1328-chris24601","name":"Chris24601","avatar":"data:image/svg+xml;base64,","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"520"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2020-06-02T11:06:42-07:00","id":1393676,"title":"","content":"V20 with it's passive dex bonus and no one turn delay on actions. Can spend blood per action and no daft restrictions on the actions beyond &quot;physical&quot; only.<br /> <br /> I personally find the idea of balancing disciplines just bonkers, all it does is suck fun out of the game.<br /> <br /> And in case anyone decides that this makes my a murder-hobo celerity junkie, of my last four characters, one has had celerity, and only at 2...","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/>Prone to being a Classic Curmudgeon, goshdarned whippersnappers...","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":3}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/1037-jamiemalk","name":"jamiemalk","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar1037_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"399"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2020-06-02T11:15:10-07:00","id":1393677,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>elmerg</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-classic-world-of-darkness/vampire-the-masquerade/1393587-old-people-which-edition-did-celerity-rules-the-best?p=1393657#post1393657\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">I'll be the odd old Methuselah. The various LARP versions, which all broke Celerity down into distinct powers, but still kept a couple of extra action tricks. You could get an initiative bonus, a bonus to ties in tests based on your speed, and up to two extra actions as well as extra steps. It worked well and I'm glad that V5 went with the various speed and dexterity-based powers in their spread.</div> </div> </div> </div>I actually kinda think *all* of the celerity rules for the TTRPG basically blow chunks -- they all end up in one of three categories: overpowered, laughable, or bland. I agree with Elmer, here -- the LARP rules were on the right path.<br /> <br /> Which brings me to my answer to the question: the best version is HOMEBREWED CELERITY!!!!!!!!<br /> <br /> Seriously, just take the discipline out of the game and replace it with something better. We've been playing this game for decades.... we know what works and what doesn't for our games.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/13136-legendre","name":"Legendre","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar13136_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Mar 2017"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"328"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2020-06-02T12:07:55-07:00","id":1393682,"title":"","content":"Using a blood for celerity dice to cause automatic dexterity successes &quot;just like potence with strength&quot; would create a much larger difference in results. Strength is normally a bit of a gradient; I can lift this much or I can climb &quot;this much wall&quot; whilst automatic dexterity successes would let you shoot the eyes of flying crows and let an unathletic vampire backflip into the open window of a moving car. <br /> <br /> Maybe, &quot;spend a blood, double your celerity dice&quot; would work But I also think celerity works best if it's a real blood drain. Like you can keep spending blood after that for one more die (Up to your celerity rating). Could be a good way to deal with Rage too.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":2}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/25794-mywifeisscary","name":"MyWifeIsScary","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar25794_2.png","title":"Banned","rank":0,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2019"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"3711"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2020-06-02T20:39:35-07:00","id":1393735,"title":"","content":"My favorites, in descending order:<br /> <br /> <b>1</b> - Requiem 2e: I know it's not VtM, but I like the execution of Celerity a lot. For those who don't know it, it has both active and passive benefits that scale with your dots. Passively, add Celerity to Defense, even against firearms. Actively, spend one blood to jump to the top of initiative for one round, multiply your speed by (Celerity+1) for one action, or pre-empt someone else's action; this last one can only be used a number of times per scene equal to your Celerity.<br /> <br /> <b>2</b> - V5: I like some of the lower-level powers (like automatically succeeding at balance checks), but I think it went too far in nerfing the mess that was V20. Needing five dots to pre-empt someone else's action is a bit too weak for my tastes.<br /> <br /> <b>3</b> - MET: Struck a good balance between &quot;multiple actions dominate everything in combat&quot; and &quot;Celerity is useless without that&quot;. Each dot unlocks a new power (just like with Dominate or Auspex or whatever); at five dots you can spend blood to get two extra actions per round, among other benefits, and there's no way to get more actions than that.<br /> <br /> <b>4</b> - DAV20: Made some progress toward fixing V20's version, but still feels awkwardly in-between in terms of power level.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/24808-draconis","name":"Draconis","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar24808_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2018"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"805"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2020-06-02T23:36:02-07:00","id":1393748,"title":"","content":"I think that in 5th edition Celirity is downgraded, but the truth is that most disciplines are in this edition. It is nearly impossible to do aggravated damage with a starting character neonate. Protean, Potence and Blood Sorcery have the superpowers of combat al level 4 or 5. Oblivion can do aggravated in 3rd level (with necromantic entropy) but is not a combat power and the target must be paralyzed. So, in this context, Celirity is not unbalanced.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":2}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/24202-justycar","name":"Justycar","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar24202_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"May 2018"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"320"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2020-06-03T03:21:05-07:00","id":1393754,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Draconis</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-classic-world-of-darkness/vampire-the-masquerade/1393587-old-people-which-edition-did-celerity-rules-the-best?p=1393735#post1393735\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">My favorites, in descending order:<br /> <br /> <b>1</b> - Requiem 2e: I know it's not VtM, but I like the execution of Celerity a lot. For those who don't know it, it has both active and passive benefits that scale with your dots. Passively, add Celerity to Defense, even against firearms. Actively, spend one blood to jump to the top of initiative for one round, multiply your speed by (Celerity+1) for one action, or pre-empt someone else's action; this last one can only be used a number of times per scene equal to your Celerity.<br /> <br /> <b>2</b> - V5: I like some of the lower-level powers (like automatically succeeding at balance checks), but I think it went too far in nerfing the mess that was V20. Needing five dots to pre-empt someone else's action is a bit too weak for my tastes.</div> </div> </div> </div>I liked Requiem's a lot too (mechanically speaking I always thought it was almost a complete improvement over Masquerade, unfortunately the setting felt bland and disjointed to me).<br /> <br /> About V5, as I said I consider the current incarnation of celerity the most ballanced of all and the easiest to manage for both the ST and the players. It's still pretty powerful without being OP. The problem is of course some of the powers presented in core are less useful than others. Generally speaking Blink is much better than Traversal, for example. As for the 5th dot powers Lightning Strike makes you capable of hitting someone with no chance for the target to defend unless he/she also possesses Celerity 5, which is pretty powerful imo.<br /> <br />","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4e","count":1}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/24992-haquim","name":"Haquim","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar24992_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Jan 2019"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"523"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2020-06-03T06:36:08-07:00","id":1393769,"title":"","content":"Requiem 2ed - Celerity is best imho, otherwise V20 if you are pro: multiple actions per round. V5 got some got ideas as it delivers options, but it would not hurt to have a passive effect like Requiem\u00b4s.","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/>Gamelines: Requiem, Lost, Awakening, Trinity Continuum: Aeon, some V5.<br /> Likes cheesecake (quark-based)","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":1}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/1305-maekkel","name":"maekkel","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar1305_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":0,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"206"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2020-06-03T08:58:57-07:00","id":1393787,"title":"","content":"1) Requiem 2nd Ed (which could be said probably for most mechanics)<br /> <br /> 2) DAV20","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":3}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/12142-undead-rabbit","name":"Undead rabbit","avatar":"data:image/svg+xml;base64,","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Jul 2016"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"888"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2020-06-03T19:07:58-07:00","id":1393900,"title":"","content":"V5, hands down. Extra actions are broken, have always been broken, and will always be broken, no matter how much they cost. The discipline simply had to do something else. So now it does. <br /> <br /> Requiem 2e\u2019s take is also a worthy fix, though I\u2019m not sad V5 breaks it up into separate powers. The two games aren\u2019t really comparable in terms of disciplines, as their design philosophy in that area is too different.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4e","count":1}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/352-black-flag","name":"Black Flag","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar352_3.png","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"541"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2020-06-03T19:32:34-07:00","id":1393916,"title":"","content":"Shameless Plug: My homebrew Celerity Rules. Maybe you, gentle reader, can find something in here that you like and adapt it to your own game, whatever version of the rules you use.<br /> <br /> <i>Note: The mechanics of this won't be immediately apparent -- combat in my game is handled with a contested roll between opponents using their &quot;Combat Values&quot; (basically the same number as their attack or dodge dice pools) and then if someone lands a blow, a follow-up contested roll of the victorious character's Damage Total (DMT -- depends on weapon, skill, strength/potence if applicable) against the losing person's Soak roll to determine if &quot;HIGH&quot; or &quot;LOW&quot; damage is inflicted.</i><br /> <br /> <span style=\"font-size:16px\"><b>Celerity Zero: &quot;Combat Celerity&quot;</b></span><br /> (Combat)<br /> By spending a Blood Point the user of this power can act with superhuman speed for a single combat turn, benefiting from an opponent\u2019s comparative sluggishness. This is the \u201cbasic\u201d advantage gained by those with the Celerity discipline, and the following rules apply:<br /> <br /> \u2022 In hand-to-hand combat (Brawl or Melee), or when evading, the user gains Advantage on CBT or DMT if he or she has one more level of active Celerity than the relevant opponent.<br /> \u2022 If the power user has 2 or more levels than the opponent, he or she gains Advantage on both CBT and DMT (if attacking).<br /> \u2022 Combat Celerity does not affect ranged combat unless the power user is evading a ranged attack, in which the same rules apply (i.e., Advantage on the EVADE CBT only).<br /> \u2022 In multi-person combat, the power user may split his or her active levels of Celerity between opponents. Once that allocation has been made, compare the numbers allocated with each opponent\u2019s respective Celerity (if any) then treat as above.<br /> <br /> Note that unless specified below, separate Blood Points must be spent to activate Combat Celerity and another Celerity power in the same round.<br /> <br /> <span style=\"font-size:16px\"><b>\u25cf Flash</b></span><br /> (Combat)<br /> The vampire with this power reacts with superhumanly fast reflexes, and may spend a Blood Point to react to any action or movement that they can reasonably sense. This power does not, however, allow a reaction if the power user is caught completely unawares. In terms of mechanics, the power user may spend a blood to negate a Surprise Attack or turn an Ambush Attack into a Surprise Attack. The power user is still helpless when Blindsided.<br /> <br /> As a fringe benefit, use of this power also allows the power user to aim a firearm (and receive aiming bonuses) in effectively zero time for the cost of a Blood Point, provided they are not engaged in hand-to-hand combat.<br /> <br /> <span style=\"font-size:16px\"><b>\u25cf\u25cf Dead Time</b></span><br /> (Fair Escape)<br /> By expending a Blood Point, the user of this power moves so fast that most others simply cannot react. While moving this fast they may only make very simple actions - such as running in a straight line, drawing a weapon, open-ing an unlocked door, or moving an object that no one is holding. For the power user, the world appears to stop. They may take simple actions for (subjectively) 6 seconds; this power may not be used in Combat except for purposes of escaping. For everyone else, no time seems to pass. Those without either Auspex or active Celerity are unable to see the power user - they are effectively invisible while in Dead Time. Those with Auspex will see a brief blur.<br /> <br /> <span style=\"font-size:16px\"><b>\u25cf\u25cf\u25cf Blur</b></span><br /> (Combat)<br /> When using Combat Celerity, the power user moves at such a rapid pace that they appear to blur. The vampire is able to move and roll with even the swiftest sword strikes, minimizing damage. Thus, the power user gains Advantage on his or her Soak rolls, as well as the other Advantages given by Combat Celerity. This does not cost any blood beyond the initial cost of activating Combat Celerity.<br /> <br /> <span style=\"font-size:16px\"><b>\u25cf\u25cf\u25cf\u25cf Foresight</b></span><br /> (Combat)<br /> The power user has developed their reflexes to such an extent that it almost seems that he or she can see a few seconds into the future. He or she may burn a Blood Point to react to any physical action her or she can sense \u2014 in any way \u2014 before that action completes. In terms of mechanics, the power user cannot be Ambushed or Surprised, but may still be Blindsided. However, if there is any advanced warning<i> at all</i> -- even the breaking of glass from a sniper's bullet coming through the window -- the power user may treat Blindsides as Ambushes.<br /> <br /> When using Combat Celerity, the power user may wait to hear what any character who does not have Foresight is doing, before deciding his or her own actions. (This does <i><b>not </b></i>take priority over Auspex 5B\u2019s similar power.) Additionally, if the power user does nothing but Evade, this power grants an <i>additional </i>claim to Advantage on all Evade rolls, and relieves the power user of the necessity to seek cover while evading ranged attacks. (You are so fast you can dodge bullets.)<br /> <br /> <span style=\"font-size:16px\"><b>\u25cf\u25cf\u25cf\u25cf\u25cf Abattoir</b></span><br /> (Combat)<br /> <b>[5A] </b>The possessor of this fearsome power has the ability to become a whirlwind of death and destruction in close combat. This power offers two distinct benefits:<br /> <br /> \u2022 Celerity ranks need not be split between multiple opponents: every opponent is allocated the power user\u2019s full discipline rank. Normal multi-party combat rules will still apply, however, except as described below.<br /> \u2022 The power user may also \u201cspend\u201d ranks of Combat Celerity each round to simply ignore the multiple opponent rules and attack/evade additional opponents without any penalty. A vampire with Celerity 5 could thus &quot;spend&quot; three ranks to engage up to four opponents without penalty, while still preserving two ranks to claim advantage against each opponent. This aspect of the power cannot be used while the power user is being grappled.<br /> <br /> <span style=\"font-size:16px\"><b>\u25cf\u25cf\u25cf\u25cf\u25cf Untouchable</b></span><br /> (Combat)<br /> <b>[5B]</b> In any combat contest in which the power user is Evading an attack and has activated Combat Celerity \u2014 including those for which he has an Evade ranking of \u201cZero\u201d or in multi-party combat \u2014 the power user may add the difference between his or her activated Celerity ranking and the attacker\u2019s activated Celerity ranking to the Evade roll.<br /> <br /> <span style=\"font-size:16px\"><b>\u25cf\u25cf\u25cf\u25cf\u25cf\u25cf Blink</b></span><br /> (Fair Escape)<br /> <b>[6A]</b> This power is essentially identical to Dead Time, but the power user moves so quickly, that he or she has <i>twenty </i>seconds of subjective time available to perform actions. This is an immense amount of time \u2014 enough to run over to a desk, scrawl a hasty note saying that you are sorry you had to leave, and to run out the door and down the stairs. It is enough time to run over to the window, carefully open the curtains to the sun, and run out the door, locking it behind you.<br /> Seriously. It\u2019s a <i><b>lot </b></i>of time. And a lot can happen in twenty seconds, change a vampires\u2019 life\u2026<br /> <br /> <span style=\"font-size:16px\"><b>\u25cf\u25cf\u25cf\u25cf\u25cf\u25cf Between the Moments</b></span><br /> (Combat)<br /> <b>[6B]</b> By burning a Blood Point, the power user may gain a \u201cfree\u201d round of combat activity between normal combat rounds during which only he or she can act. The power is activated during one round, and the power user receives the &quot;free&quot; round after that round ends. Opponents may still contest using their Brawl or Melee CBTs (as appropriate) but will not cause any damage, and unless they possess either Celerity (at any rank) or Auspex (at rank 4 or higher) their combat values are <b><u>zero</u></b>. The power user gains the benefits of his or her Combat Celerity during this \u201cfree\u201d round, without the necessity of spending any additional blood.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/13136-legendre","name":"Legendre","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar13136_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Mar 2017"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"328"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2020-06-04T08:01:48-07:00","id":1394030,"title":"","content":"I like the V20 version, for its simplicity and versatility, while still being less broken than the earlier one. The DAV20 version is good too, but it's more fiddly and yes, dependent on the DAV multilple action rules. I refuse to waste time speaking about the V5 version.","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/>If nothing worked, then let's think!","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":6}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/6363-pm\u00e1rk","name":"PM\u00e1rk","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar6363_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Jun 2015"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"2363"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2020-06-04T08:38:14-07:00","id":1394043,"title":"","content":"I think the V20 version is the better one.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":4}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/24832-blailton","name":"blailton","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar24832_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2018"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"389"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2020-06-04T12:05:29-07:00","id":1394106,"title":"","content":"This is what i do<br /> <br /> <b>Passive effects</b><br /> Adds your celerity rating +1 to dexterity<br /> <br /> <b>Active effects</b><br /> <b>Turbo:</b>Double your speed rating by spending a blood point for the rest of the scene by doing so it\u00b4s more easy to chase your enemies or escape from them<br /> <b>Dead Time:</b>Spend one blood point and you can perform two actions instead of one during the turn , this can only be activated a max number of times equal to your celerity rating during a scene but a celerity 5 devotion allows for unlimited usage","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/><a href=\"https://www.deviantart.com/cicerondixit/gallery\" target=\"_blank\">https://www.deviantart.com/cicerondixit/gallery</a>","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":1}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/24037-leandro16","name":"Leandro16","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar24037_2.png","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Mar 2018"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"552"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2020-06-05T06:23:44-07:00","id":1394314,"title":"","content":"my fave is DAV20, but that's due to two things:<br /> <br /> 1) The amount of celerity attacks is halved, which strikes a good balance between only having to spend one blood point, but not being able to push a character straight out of CG that can crush the anything.<br /> 2) The multiple-action rules of DAV20, which go (kind of) back to Revised. The reason why I like the DAV20 rules for multiple actions is simple: It accounts for Automatic/Reflexive actions in your multiple action pool.<br /> <br /> I have no idea why they went back to the 2nd ed rules with all the other 20th gamelines, but split-dice-pools simply does not account for automatic actions. How do you split your dice pool when your first action is drawing a weapon, and the second one is using it? <img src=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/images/smilies/smile.png\" border=\"0\" alt=\"\" title=\"Smile\" smilieid=\"1\" class=\"inlineimg\" /> It's not in the rules. A massive oversight.","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/><a href=\"https://is.gd/wodchart\" target=\"_blank\">cWoD Dice Probability Chart</a> ||| <a href=\"https://is.gd/wodcalc\" target=\"_blank\">cWoD Dice Statistics Calculator</a> ||| <a href=\"https://is.gd/wodarmor\" target=\"_blank\">cWoD Alternative Armor System</a><br /> <a href=\"https://is.gd/woddamage\" target=\"_blank\">cWoD Alternative Damage Roll System</a> ||| <a href=\"https://is.gd/woddamage2\" target=\"_blank\">My explanation of cWoD Damage Levels</a> ||| <a href=\"https://is.gd/wodstrength\" target=\"_blank\">'Interesting' Strength Attribute Stuff</a><br /> <a href=\"https://is.gd/wodboom\" target=\"_blank\">EXPLOSIVE cWoD STUFF!</a> ||| <a href=\"https://is.gd/wodheresy\" target=\"_blank\">How Technocrats don't think they are Mages</a>","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/6606-ambrosia","name":"Ambrosia","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar6606_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Jun 2015"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"1567"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2020-06-07T14:37:37-07:00","id":1394853,"title":"","content":"V20 is my favorite. Passive benefits, spend a blood point per extra physical action, extra actions are usable in the same turn rather than the following one.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":3}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/3505-matt-the-bruins-fan","name":"Matt the Bruins fan","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar3505_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Jul 2014"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"2401"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2020-06-07T16:46:20-07:00","id":1394873,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Ambrosia</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-classic-world-of-darkness/vampire-the-masquerade/1393587-old-people-which-edition-did-celerity-rules-the-best?p=1394314#post1394314\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">I have no idea why they went back to the 2nd ed rules with all the other 20th gamelines, but split-dice-pools simply does not account for automatic actions. How do you split your dice pool when your first action is drawing a weapon, and the second one is using it? <img src=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/images/smilies/smile.png\" border=\"0\" alt=\"\" title=\"Smile\" smilieid=\"1\" class=\"inlineimg\" /> It's not in the rules. A massive oversight.</div> </div> </div> </div>My house rule fix is that automatic actions don\u2019t count for determining the size of your dice pool for the turn, but do take a die to perform. So drawing and firing is your normal dice pool for the attack -1 die.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/1328-chris24601","name":"Chris24601","avatar":"data:image/svg+xml;base64,","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"520"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2020-06-17T00:05:37-07:00","id":1397014,"title":"","content":"I actually prefer the DAV20 approach, but I don't think it's mutually exclusive with certain other depictions of Celerity. I never cared for 'spend one blood point and get a ton of actions with full dice pool' but I didnt mind spending 'one BP per extra action with full dice pool' I remember from the original DAV either. I think it really comes down to what you see as the source of vampiric speed. If its simply moving faster/imparting greater force to your body then the DAV20 approach works great. If you see it as temporal manipulation (speed up/slow down time relative to others) then the 'tradtiononal' celerity works fine too. Given that celerity is a 'half physical/half metaphysical' like the other physical disciplines like fortitude and Potence and the fact celerity can have features that involve both temporal manipulation and physical force there's not really any reason to pick one over the other (And I never did. I also happen to like 5th's view on Celerity because it gives you more versatility in how to use it with your character. Besides I also felt that vampires should be able to get bonuses to their mental or social attributes from certain disciplines, just like they could have used blood to enhance them. I liked that part about 5th edition too.) <br />","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/25533-mister_dunpeal","name":"Mister_Dunpeal","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar25533_2.png","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Jul 2019"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"308"}]},"thankedby":[]}]}